  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat comes not from a single breakthrough but from two compounding curves: endurance (how long one agent can work autonomously, citing METR's time-horizon doubling data) and breadth (how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate, citing Anthropic's disclosure of the GTG-1002 espionage campaign using Claude Code). Its key points are that attackers exploit stolen access so the victim pays the compute bill (the \"LLMjacking\" problem described by Sysdig), that price and usage gates fail because they filter buyers rather than tenants, and that swarms of cheap attempts make even low per-attempt success rates dangerous in aggregate. The intended audience is engineering and security teams who consider agentic threats overhyped, and the tone is an opinionated analytical essay with a polemical edge, supported by charts, formulas, and concrete citations. Its recurring framing device is the Terminator franchise: the line \"absolutely will not stop\" is reinterpreted as \"absolutely will not stop billing,\" and Skynet-as-distributed-system is used to represent orchestration.",
